75 #edb/ref 17592186478611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186478940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186478611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479038 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4f7da20-bbae-51ed-a0e8-82abd9017a31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy-4}$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "192e8101-5b84-55a9-943c-4057db856b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479065 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy-4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{x\\cdot {x}^{2}-4}}=\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={e}^{2x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "889d0bfa-8294-56c7-a3ce-0b0d572aff69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479104 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d50078aa-bdfd-5339-beac-094a7cdda30c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479133 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+({e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =s(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){e}^{s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
